---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Content as Policy } from "../../POLICY.md";
import { site } from "../lib/data.ts";

const { config } = site();
---

<Layout title="About" current="about" description="What Decompendium lists, how progress is measured, and how to opt out.">
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>About Decompendium</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Decompendium is a directory of matching decompilations: projects whose build reproduces a game's original
      binary byte for byte. It exists so people can see what already exists for a game before starting something new.
    </p>

    <h2>How progress is measured</h2>
    <p>
      Every {config.thresholds.stale_after_days > 1 ? "few hours" : "day"}, Decompendium fetches each project's latest report
      from its own CI. Only successful builds of the default branch count; pull requests and forks are ignored. Numbers
      are shown as reported, never entered by hand.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Matched code</strong>: bytes of code that compile to exactly the original instructions.</li>
      <li><strong>Fully linked</strong>: code in translation units that are complete and linked from source.</li>
      <li><strong>Functions</strong>: whole functions that match.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Nothing here is ranked by percentage. Projects differ in size, toolchain and goals, and a number alone
      doesn't capture that.
    </p>

    <h2>Opting out</h2>
    <p>
      Every project page has an opt-out link. Maintainers can hide a project from the home page, or remove it
      entirely within 48 hours.
    </p>

    <div class="policy"><Policy /></div>
  </div>
</Layout>
